{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"SPLIT (2026-08-07). This ticket was too large and its plan review never converged: four rounds\nproduced 2 -> 7 -> 4 -> 7 blocking findings. The pattern was structural, not editorial — every verb\nthe ticket touched dragged its own unenumerated consumer set (workflows, pressure-test scripts,\ncanary wiring, contract tests, docs), so remediating one verb's findings surfaced the next verb's.\nSplitting it makes each consumer set reviewable on its own.\n\nThis ticket is RESCOPED to the surface itself — registering the `bridge` noun and its subcommand\ndispatch, plus the two verbs with no predecessor to rename from (`preview`, `sync`). It keeps: the\nscoped functional ACs (preview performs no Jira write and no ticket-event write; sync mutates), the\nB1 note that pause/resume/status behavioural assertions belong to `1104-93eb-6ce0-4a19` /\n`b69c-5787-81b8-4052` because an edge back would be a cycle, the B4 decision that `mode.py`'s\nMODE_CAPS and Mode ladder SURVIVE, and the corrected doctor AC (the bridge health verb is\n`bridge fsck`; the EXISTING top-level `rebar doctor` store link-repair command at\n`_cli/__init__.py:323` is preserved, with a test asserting it still dispatches).\n\nThe six existing `depends_on` edges into this ticket (`1104`, `424f`, `030f`, `b69c`, `9895`,\n`f789`) are unchanged and remain correct — they attach their verbs to the surface this ticket\ncreates.\n\nCarved out:\n\n* `c61a-9f8f-8ef9-4601` — \"Retire the two dead bridge verbs (bridge-status, purge-bridge)\".\n  Pure dead-code deletion, NO dependency on any sibling; can land immediately. Carries the B6\n  user-facing-string cleanup (`_engine_support/bridge.py:32`'s `_USAGE`,\n  `schemas/bridge_status.schema.json`) and the dangling \"epic 3a03\" citation, which dies with the\n  module.\n* `4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51` — \"Retire the reconcile verb and the --mode flag (CLI + engine)\".\n  `depends_on` this ticket. Carries B3 (the `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:262`\n  epic-3006 rollback-brake migration + the same-change `workflow_dispatch` input rename, with the\n  full mode -> new-surface mapping), B2 (the four direct engine callers in\n  `scripts/jira-pressure-test/`), B5 (`--filter-local-ids` -> `--only` in those same scripts), the\n  B3 test-consumer MIGRATION list, and the surface-scope note that the library\n  `rebar.reconcile(mode=)` and the MCP `reconcile` tool are retired by `f789`, not here.\n* `e27c-5a20-cd13-43b2` — \"Rename the three surviving bridge verbs onto the bridge noun\"\n  (`bridge-fsck` -> `bridge fsck`, `bridge-probe` -> `bridge check-access`, `jira-onboard` ->\n  `bridge setup`). `depends_on` this ticket. States explicitly that `check-access` and `setup` are\n  RENAMES of shipping code, not new features — which is what the reviews kept catching — and\n  enumerates each verb's consumer set (freshly grepped, including the string-named sites a symbol\n  search misses). Keeps the decision that `setup` calls `check-access` as its final validation step\n  and the rationale for `check-access` being its own verb (all three narrowing-flag precedents are\n  read-only; this creates and deletes a real Jira issue).\n\nNew edges: `4a44` depends_on this; `e27c` depends_on this; `030f-c14f-596a-4f79` depends_on `e27c`\n(030f edits bridge_fsck, which e27c renames); `f789-3f9d-8b35-4e0d` depends_on `4a44` (f789 retires\nthe library/MCP reconcile that 4a44 retires on the CLI). `c61a` has no edges by design."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786095805955605001,"uuid":"304de476-1ff1-41fd-8690-cc0c7921a79e"}